1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,397
Welcome to School Computer Science,
Explained. This is AP Computer Science

2
00:00:05,397 --> 00:00:11,377
Principles, Big Idea 3, part three. We'll
search lists fast, and organize programs

3
00:00:11,377 --> 00:00:12,544
with procedures.

4
00:00:13,717 --> 00:00:16,960
Lots of list problems need you to look at
every item.

5
00:00:17,410 --> 00:00:19,180
That's called traversing the list.

6
00:00:20,330 --> 00:00:26,051
In AP pseudocode, FOR EACH item IN list
runs the block once for every element, in

7
00:00:26,051 --> 00:00:26,474
order.

8
00:00:26,924 --> 00:00:29,463
Each time, item holds the next element.

9
00:00:30,613 --> 00:00:36,202
Here's a sum. total gets zero. FOR EACH n
IN nums, total gets total plus n.

10
00:00:36,652 --> 00:00:41,538
With nums four, seven, two, total becomes
four, then eleven, then thirteen.

11
00:00:42,688 --> 00:00:48,654
You can also count matches. count gets
zero. FOR EACH n IN nums, IF n is greater

12
00:00:48,654 --> 00:00:51,413
than five, count gets count plus one.

13
00:00:51,863 --> 00:00:55,788
For four, seven, two, only seven matches,
so count is one.

14
00:00:56,938 --> 00:01:02,032
A linear search, or sequential search,
checks each item in order until it finds

15
00:01:02,032 --> 00:01:02,741
the target.

16
00:01:03,191 --> 00:01:06,028
In the worst case, it checks every single
item.

17
00:01:07,178 --> 00:01:12,785
A traversal can also be complete or
partial. A partial traversal stops early,

18
00:01:12,785 --> 00:01:15,989
for example, as soon as the target is
found.

19
00:01:17,139 --> 00:01:23,839
Your turn. nums gets three, eight, five,
ten. total gets zero. FOR EACH n IN nums,

20
00:01:23,839 --> 00:01:29,150
IF n MOD two equals zero, total gets total
plus n. What is total?

21
00:01:29,600 --> 00:01:30,880
Pause the video now.

22
00:01:31,330 --> 00:01:35,276
Only the even numbers are added: eight
plus ten is eighteen.

23
00:01:35,726 --> 00:01:38,862
Next, a much faster way to search: binary
search.

24
00:01:40,013 --> 00:01:44,024
Searching a million items one by one could
take a million steps.

25
00:01:44,474 --> 00:01:46,948
Binary search can do it in about twenty.

26
00:01:48,098 --> 00:01:53,795
Binary search only works on sorted data.
That's the key requirement to remember for

27
00:01:53,795 --> 00:01:54,413
the exam.

28
00:01:55,563 --> 00:01:58,486
Check the middle item. If it's the target,
you're done.

29
00:01:58,936 --> 00:02:04,233
If the target is smaller, throw away the
right half. If it's bigger, throw away the

30
00:02:04,233 --> 00:02:06,658
left half. Then repeat on what's left.

31
00:02:07,808 --> 00:02:11,670
Let's find thirty seven in a sorted list
of fifteen numbers.

32
00:02:12,120 --> 00:02:17,549
The middle is twenty two. Thirty seven is
bigger, so keep the right half. The middle

33
00:02:17,549 --> 00:02:23,042
of that is forty one. Smaller, so go left.
Then thirty seven. Found, in three checks.

34
00:02:24,192 --> 00:02:30,256
Each check cuts the list in half. So for n
items, binary search takes about log base

35
00:02:30,256 --> 00:02:31,339
two of n steps.

36
00:02:31,789 --> 00:02:36,456
For a thousand items, that's about ten
checks. A linear search could take a

37
00:02:36,456 --> 00:02:37,016
thousand.

38
00:02:38,166 --> 00:02:42,957
Binary search is often much faster than
linear search, but if the data isn't

39
00:02:42,957 --> 00:02:45,227
sorted, you'd have to sort it first.

40
00:02:46,377 --> 00:02:51,611
Your turn. About how many checks does
binary search need, at most, for a sorted

41
00:02:51,611 --> 00:02:53,268
list of sixty four items?

42
00:02:53,718 --> 00:02:54,998
Pause the video now.

43
00:02:55,448 --> 00:03:01,466
Sixty four halves to one in six steps, so
about six or seven checks. A linear search

44
00:03:01,466 --> 00:03:03,042
could need sixty four.

45
00:03:03,492 --> 00:03:05,199
Next, we'll call procedures.

46
00:03:06,349 --> 00:03:08,717
You don't have to write every step
yourself.

47
00:03:09,167 --> 00:03:12,367
Procedures let you reuse code that already
exists.

48
00:03:13,517 --> 00:03:18,484
A procedure is a named group of
instructions. It may take parameters, and

49
00:03:18,484 --> 00:03:19,981
it may return a value.

50
00:03:20,431 --> 00:03:23,375
Other languages call them functions or
methods.

51
00:03:24,525 --> 00:03:28,770
To call a procedure, write its name with
arguments in parentheses.

52
00:03:29,220 --> 00:03:34,684
DISPLAY, open paren, hello, close paren,
calls the DISPLAY procedure with the

53
00:03:34,684 --> 00:03:35,748
argument hello.

54
00:03:36,898 --> 00:03:41,592
When a procedure is called, the program
jumps to it, runs its steps, then comes

55
00:03:41,592 --> 00:03:43,256
back to where it was called.

56
00:03:44,406 --> 00:03:49,419
Some procedures return a value, so you can
store it or use it in an expression.

57
00:03:49,869 --> 00:03:55,855
x gets RANDOM one comma six stores the
value returned by RANDOM. y gets INPUT

58
00:03:55,855 --> 00:03:57,954
stores what the user types.

59
00:03:59,104 --> 00:04:04,269
On the exam, you can use a procedure just
by knowing what it does, without seeing

60
00:04:04,269 --> 00:04:04,843
its code.

61
00:04:05,293 --> 00:04:07,917
Its name, parameters and description are
enough.

62
00:04:09,067 --> 00:04:15,045
Your turn. The procedure max, a comma b,
returns the larger of a and b. What is

63
00:04:15,045 --> 00:04:17,920
max, three comma max, nine comma five?

64
00:04:18,370 --> 00:04:19,650
Pause the video now.

65
00:04:20,100 --> 00:04:24,751
The inner call returns nine. Then max of
three and nine returns nine.

66
00:04:25,201 --> 00:04:28,316
Next, we'll write our own procedures, with
parameters.

67
00:04:29,465 --> 00:04:33,668
Calling procedures is useful. Writing your
own is even better.

68
00:04:34,118 --> 00:04:37,104
Let's define procedures in AP pseudocode.

69
00:04:38,254 --> 00:04:43,545
PROCEDURE, name, then parameters in
parentheses, then a block in curly braces.

70
00:04:43,995 --> 00:04:49,896
PROCEDURE greet, open paren, name, close
paren. In the block, DISPLAY hello, then

71
00:04:49,896 --> 00:04:50,843
DISPLAY name.

72
00:04:51,993 --> 00:04:56,877
Parameters are input variables. Their
values come from the arguments in each

73
00:04:56,877 --> 00:04:57,198
call.

74
00:04:57,648 --> 00:05:00,955
greet of Ana runs the block with name
equal to Ana.

75
00:05:02,105 --> 00:05:06,009
RETURN sends a value back, and ends the
procedure right away.

76
00:05:06,459 --> 00:05:12,788
PROCEDURE square, x. RETURN x times x.
Then y gets square of four makes y equal

77
00:05:12,788 --> 00:05:13,670
to sixteen.

78
00:05:14,820 --> 00:05:20,099
Procedures with parameters are general.
One procedure can handle many different

79
00:05:20,099 --> 00:05:23,908
values, instead of writing the same code
again and again.

80
00:05:25,058 --> 00:05:29,538
A good procedure does one clear job, with
a name that says what it does.

81
00:05:30,688 --> 00:05:36,807
Your turn. PROCEDURE f, a comma b. IF a is
greater than b, RETURN a minus b.

82
00:05:36,807 --> 00:05:41,397
Otherwise RETURN b minus a. What is f of
three comma ten?

83
00:05:41,847 --> 00:05:43,127
Pause the video now.

84
00:05:43,577 --> 00:05:48,569
Three is not greater than ten, so it
returns ten minus three, which is seven.

85
00:05:49,019 --> 00:05:53,094
Next, we'll see why procedures are a
powerful kind of abstraction.

86
00:05:54,244 --> 00:05:57,636
You can drive a car without knowing how
the engine works.

87
00:05:58,086 --> 00:06:00,881
Procedures let programmers work the same
way.

88
00:06:02,031 --> 00:06:07,274
Procedural abstraction means using a
procedure by knowing what it does, not how

89
00:06:07,274 --> 00:06:08,004
it does it.

90
00:06:08,454 --> 00:06:11,761
The details are hidden behind its name and
parameters.

91
00:06:12,911 --> 00:06:18,192
This helps manage complexity. A big
program is broken into procedures, each

92
00:06:18,192 --> 00:06:20,164
solving one smaller problem.

93
00:06:20,614 --> 00:06:21,915
That's modularity.

94
00:06:23,065 --> 00:06:28,728
Procedures also reduce repeated code. If
the same steps appear in many places, put

95
00:06:28,728 --> 00:06:30,937
them in a procedure and call it.

96
00:06:32,087 --> 00:06:36,963
And if you improve the inside of a
procedure, every part of the program that

97
00:06:36,963 --> 00:06:40,813
calls it gets the improvement, without
changing those calls.

98
00:06:41,263 --> 00:06:45,508
As long as it still does the same job,
nothing else needs to change.

99
00:06:46,658 --> 00:06:52,688
On the exam, look for answers that mention
reusing code, hiding details, or making a

100
00:06:52,688 --> 00:06:54,914
program easier to read and fix.

101
00:06:56,064 --> 00:07:01,738
Your turn. A game draws a star in ten
places using the same twelve lines of code

102
00:07:01,738 --> 00:07:04,149
each time. What's a better design?

103
00:07:04,599 --> 00:07:05,879
Pause the video now.

104
00:07:06,329 --> 00:07:11,197
Write a procedure, drawStar, with
parameters for the position, and call it

105
00:07:11,197 --> 00:07:11,855
ten times.

106
00:07:12,305 --> 00:07:16,123
Next, we'll use libraries, random values,
and simulations.

107
00:07:17,273 --> 00:07:21,950
If this helped, subscribe to School
Computer Science, Explained, so you don't

108
00:07:21,950 --> 00:07:23,225
miss the next lesson.

109
00:07:23,675 --> 00:07:24,571
See you there.

